<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union Minière du Haut Katanga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1906">1906</a>–<a href="/years/1967">1967</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Union Minière</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Union Minière du Haut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a> was a Belgian mining company founded in 1906 to exploit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> of Katanga in the Belgian Congo. It became one of the world's largest producers of copper, cobalt and uranium. It supported the secession of Katanga in 1960–1963, and its Congolese assets were nationalised in 1967, becoming Gécamines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-miniere-du-haut-katanga.jpg" alt="Union Minière du Haut Katanga" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boubloub · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HautKatangaHQ.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The company was founded as a joint venture of the Belgian Compagnie du <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>, itself a subsidiary of the Compagnie du Congo pour le Commerce et l'Industrie, the Comité Spécial du Katanga and Tanganyika Concessions Limited. With the backing of the colonial state it received a concession of 20,000 square kilometres in Katanga. By the start of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/societe-generale-de-belgique">Société Générale de Belgique</a>, which controlled about 70 percent of the Congolese economy, effectively controlled the company.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Railways, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benguela">Benguela</a> railway to the Angolan coast, opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>'s minerals to export. The Ruashi Mine began work in 1911 and produced 997 tonnes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> in its first year; by 1919 output had reached 22,000 tonnes a year. Besides copper the company mined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, cobalt, radium, uranium, zinc and other metals, and in the 1950s it accounted for 75 percent of the world's cobalt. In 1919 it formed the Société Générale Métallurgique d'Hoboken, whose plant at Olen was the first to produce radium industrially.</p><p>From 1922 it refined uranium, and by 1926 it held a near monopoly of the world market. At a meeting on 18 September 1942 its head, Edgar Sengier, sold General Kenneth Nichols of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a> the 1,500 tonnes of uranium, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinkolobwe">Shinkolobwe</a> mine, that the project needed; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> obtained the uranium for the atomic bomb from the company.</p><p>The company ran schools, dispensaries and hospitals and held almost governmental power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/katanga">Katanga</a>; in 1959 export duties it paid made up half the colony's revenue. After independence in June 1960, fearing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patrice-lumumba">Patrice Lumumba</a> would nationalise its holdings, it backed Moïse Tshombe's secessionist State of Katanga and paid 1.25 billion Belgian francs, an advance on 1960 taxes owed to Lumumba's government, into Tshombe's account. The secession ended in 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>President Joseph-Désiré Mobutu's government took over all its possessions and activities in the Congo, forming the state company Gécamines, and in May 1967 the company cancelled the Congolese state's shareholding and turned to work outside the Congo. Gécamines' output later fell by as much as 70%. In 1968 the company became Union Minière, which after a merger of 1989 took the name Umicore in 2001.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Union Minière du Haut-Katanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Union Minière du Haut Katanga</b> mined copper and cobalt, and uranium from Shinkolobwe supplied the Manhattan Project.</blockquote><a href="/state/belgian-congo">Belgian Congo</a></li></ul></section>
